# The demo's public entry point (`demo-infrastructure` D7). This site block is
# the whole of what the box exposes: the app and the index publish no port and
# have no interface outside the shared network namespace (D1), so nothing else
# can be reached even by mistake.
#
# The local rehearsal uses Caddyfile.local instead; keep the two bodies the same
# or the rehearsal stops rehearsing anything.

models.masamaeda.com {
	# There is no `tls` line on purpose. Caddy obtains the certificate from
	# Let's Encrypt (ZeroSSL as fallback), renews it, and redirects http:// to
	# https:// by itself — no operator step issues or installs anything. Its
	# /data is a named volume (compose.yaml) so a rebuild or a rollback re-uses
	# the certificate it already has: Let's Encrypt allows five duplicate
	# issuances a week, which a rebuild loop would otherwise spend in an hour.
	#
	# HTTP/2 comes with TLS, and stock Caddy also serves HTTP/3 on 443/udp,
	# which compose.yaml publishes. That is the lever the demo notes name for an
	# ocean of latency: a first screen is ~114 small requests, and multiplexing
	# is the cheapest answer to paying a round trip for each of them.

	encode zstd gzip

	# A cap on what the API will read, in front of the app's own
	# (`maxRequestBodySize` in server/src/index.ts). Deliberately the same
	# number, so a body refused here would have been refused there and the two
	# cannot disagree about what is too big. The largest legitimate body is
	# `POST /api/semantic/poses` — up to 1024 library paths, ~105–205 KB for
	# real libraries. Over the cap answers 413.
	@api path /api/*
	request_body @api {
		max_size 1MB
	}

	# `Host` passes through unchanged — there is no `header_up Host` here, and
	# there must not be. What the app answers is decided by the origins in the
	# committed configuration (deploy/demo/config.json) against the header the
	# visitor sent; rewriting it here would move that decision into the proxy,
	# silently, and the guard's refusals would stop meaning anything. It is also
	# what makes the runbook's liveness check work: a foreign `Origin` reaches
	# the app and is refused, while a foreign `Host` never gets past this block
	# in the first place.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3177
}

# **No rate limit, deliberately** (D7). Stock Caddy has none. The index
# serialises queries, so a flood queues rather than overloads, and the flat walk
# is bounded by its own step budget: abuse degrades to waiting rather than to
# failure. Nginx was weighed for its built-in `limit_req` and declined — the
# feature that matters daily is the certificate above, and Caddy's needs no
# configuration.
#
# If that stops being true, the route is the `caddy-ratelimit` plugin
# (github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit), which the stock image cannot load: it
# needs a builder stage, roughly
#
#     FROM caddy:2-builder AS builder
#     RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit
#     FROM caddy:2
#     COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
#
# and a `rate_limit` block in the site above. Recorded here so the next operator
# does not go looking for a knob this image does not have.
